<description>
Focusing with an STL mirror
===========================

This example demonstrates interoperability with CAD models using STL_.
The STL format describes surface geometry as a collection of triangles.
It is lightweight and lets us exchange models without heavy CAD
dependencies. STL support in xrt relies on the ``numpy-stl`` package.
Make sure it is installed in the Python environment used to run this
example:

.. code-block:: bash

   pip install numpy-stl

.. _STL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/STL_(file_format)

This example compares an analytical ``ToroidMirror`` with a surface
reconstructed from an STL file by ``MeshOE``. It shows how the choice of
surface hints and the sampling of the exported geometry affect focusing.

The template contains two matching source-mirror-screen configurations.
Both sources have the same settings, and the mirrors and screens have the
same relative positions and orientations. The STL branch is translated by
30 mm along X so that both configurations can be viewed together.
The analytical mirror, ``toroidMirror01``, is the reference; the imported
mirror, ``toroidMirrorSTL``, is the element we will modify.

1. Start with a flat surface
----------------------------

Open ``toroid_stl2.xml`` from ``examples/withRaycing/17_STL`` in xrtQook
and view the beamline in xrtGlow. If you already have the template open,
you can continue there.

Start with ``fileName = None`` on ``toroidMirrorSTL``. If an STL is already
loaded, clear the file using the clear button beside the filename.
Without a loaded file, ``MeshOE`` uses a flat surface, regardless of its
selected surface hints. The reflected beam in this branch remains
divergent, while the analytical mirror focuses its beam.

Set the color source in xrtGlow to ``xprime`` and disable **Global color
span**. The rainbow shows the horizontal ray angles and makes the
divergence easy to see. Keep these settings and the same scene view for
the following comparisons.

.. figure:: _images/17_1_stl_flat_start.png
   :width: 100%
   :alt: Analytical mirror focusing on the left and flat MeshOE diverging on the right.

   The analytical and flat branches, colored by horizontal ray angle.

2. Load the coarse STL
----------------------

Center the scene on ``toroidMirrorSTL`` and double-click it to open its
inspector. Double-click the value beside ``fileName`` to open the file
editor, then browse to ``toroidMirrorSTL.stl`` in the example directory.
This file is approximately 122 KiB and contains a 25 x 25 vertex grid.
Keep ``orientation = XYZ`` and ``recenter = True``.

For this first comparison, leave both ``surfaceHintX`` and ``surfaceHintY``
set to ``parabolic``. Open the ``screen_toroid`` and ``screen_STL`` plots
side by side. Compare the focal spots and their reported widths using the
same axis limits. The spots differ, even though the imported mirror looks
like the analytical one in the scene.

Check the **Diagnostic** parameters in the ``toroidMirrorSTL`` inspector:
``RsagFit`` (sagittal radius in mm), ``RmerFit`` (meridional radius in mm),
and ``fitRmsError`` (RMS height residual in micrometres). Compare the radii
with the reference values of 50 mm and 200 mm. Note the fit error for
comparison with the next step.

.. figure:: _images/17_2_stl_toro_25.png
   :width: 100%
   :alt: Analytical mirror and coarse STL mirror in the same scene view.

   The 25 x 25 STL looks plausible in the scene, but focuses differently.

.. list-table:: Analytical reference versus the coarse STL
   :widths: 50 50
   :header-rows: 1

   * - Analytical ``ToroidMirror``
     - 25 x 25 STL, both hints parabolic
   * - .. image:: _images/17_2_toroid.png
          :width: 100%
          :alt: Focal spot from the analytical ToroidMirror.
     - .. image:: _images/17_2_STL_25.png
          :width: 100%
          :alt: Focal spot from the 25 x 25 STL with parabolic X and Y hints.

.. figure:: _images/17_2_diag.png
   :width: 400px
   :alt: Fit diagnostics for the coarse STL with parabolic X and Y hints.

   The coarse STL's fitted radii and RMS height residual.

3. Increase the sampling and match the profiles
-----------------------------------------------

To export a more densely sampled STL model, open the **Rendering** panel
and change **OE tessellation X** and **OE tessellation Y** from 25 to 100
points along each dimension. Hover over the analytical ``toroidMirror01``
in the scene and make sure it is highlighted. Right-click and choose
**File &gt; Export OE shape to STL**, then save the model as an STL file.
The exported model can also be viewed and modified using third-party 3D
tools.

For this example, we already provide ``toroidMirrorSTL100.stl``, created
in a similar manner. The suffix refers to its 100 x 100 vertex grid.
Load this file into ``toroidMirrorSTL``. First keep both hints set to
``parabolic`` and observe the ``screen_STL`` spot. Then change the hints
to:

* ``surfaceHintX = circular`` for the sagittal profile;
* ``surfaceHintY = parabolic`` for the meridional profile.

These settings match the surface used by the analytical ``ToroidMirror``
in this example: its X profile is circular, while its Y profile is
parabolic.

Using ``parabolic`` on both axes or ``circular`` on both axes gives a worse
fit than the matching circular-X/parabolic-Y combination. The difference
between the circular and parabolic profiles along Y may be almost
imperceptible in the scene or focal spot, but the optimizer fits the full
surface over all selected STL vertices. A mismatch across that surface
can shift the fitted parameters and affect focusing. Matching circular
hints also select a coupled toroidal surface, rather than the independent
circular X and parabolic Y profiles used by the reference mirror.

Compare the dense STL's focal spot before and after changing the X hint,
as shown below. The circular X profile brings the spot width much closer
to the analytical reference from step 2. The denser mesh and the matching
profiles provide a better reconstruction of the analytical surface.

Check ``RsagFit``, ``RmerFit``, and ``fitRmsError`` again. The fitted radii
should now be close to 50 mm and 200 mm, and the RMS height residual should
be smaller than in step 2. Compare these diagnostics alongside the focal
spots: the height residual describes the surface fit, while the screen
plots show its effect on focusing.

.. figure:: _images/17_3_stl_toro_100.png
   :width: 100%
   :alt: Analytical and dense STL mirrors producing similar focusing in xrtGlow.

   The dense STL with circular X and parabolic Y hints, viewed as in step 1.

.. list-table:: Dense STL: effect of the sagittal hint
   :widths: 50 50
   :header-rows: 1

   * - X parabolic, Y parabolic
     - X circular, Y parabolic
   * - .. image:: _images/17_3_stl_para.png
          :width: 100%
          :alt: Dense STL focal spot with both hints parabolic.
     - .. image:: _images/17_3_stl_para_circ.png
          :width: 100%
          :alt: Dense STL focal spot with circular X and parabolic Y hints.

.. figure:: _images/17_3_diag.png
   :width: 400px
   :alt: Fit diagnostics for the dense STL with circular X and parabolic Y hints.

   The matching profiles recover radii close to those of the reference mirror.

4. Explore the effect of the illuminated footprint
--------------------------------------------------

On both ``geometricSource01`` and ``geometricSource02``, reduce ``dxprime``
from ``0.01`` to ``0.001``. This reduces the horizontal angular spread by
a factor of ten. Keep the other source parameters unchanged so that the
two branches remain comparable.

With the dense STL still loaded, keep ``surfaceHintY = parabolic`` and
switch ``surfaceHintX`` between ``parabolic`` and ``circular``. Compare
``screen_STL`` with ``screen_toroid`` after each change.

The narrower beam illuminates a smaller region around the mirror center,
where a parabolic profile more closely approximates a circular one. The
agreement therefore improves, and the difference between the two X hints
becomes less pronounced. The circular X hint still matches the reference
profile. Restore ``dxprime = 0.01`` on both sources to make the effect of
the larger footprint visible again.

What this comparison shows
--------------------------

A similar-looking surface is not enough to guarantee the same focal spot.
Ray tracing uses the reconstructed surface and its normals; small errors
in the surface slopes can change the reflected ray directions appreciably.
A parabolic approximation departs increasingly from a circular profile
away from the center, so the illuminated footprint matters.

The STL must also sample the geometry sufficiently well. Increasing the
vertex density can improve reconstruction, but it does not replace choosing
a suitable surface model. Compare the focal spots as well as the displayed
shape, and use the fit diagnostics to help assess the imported surface.
</description>
